  "content": {
    "id": "p29-q15-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "15",
    "prompt": "Suppose in a social club, there are $n$ groups of $k$ people in each group.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p29-q15-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "How many ways can two people be selected from the social club?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\binom{nk}{2}$ ways.",
          "worked": "There are $nk$ people in the club. Selecting an unordered pair gives $\\binom{nk}{2}$ ways.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 12
      },
      {
        "id": "p29-q15-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Consider the cases of the two people being in the same group, or not being in the same group. Show that\n$$\\binom{nk}{2}=n\\binom{k}{2}+k^2\\binom{n}{2}.$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\binom{nk}{2}=n\\binom{k}{2}+k^2\\binom{n}{2}$.\n\nThe two terms count pairs from the same group and from different groups respectively.",
          "worked": "Same group: choose one of the $n$ groups, then choose two of its $k$ people. This gives $n\\binom{k}{2}$ pairs.\n\nDifferent groups: choose two groups in $\\binom{n}{2}$ ways, then one person from each in $k^2$ ways. This gives $k^2\\binom{n}{2}$ pairs.\n\nThese cases are mutually exclusive and cover every pair. By part (a),\n$$\\binom{nk}{2}=n\\binom{k}{2}+k^2\\binom{n}{2}.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 52
      }
    ]
  },
